"""One existing purchase-cost owner refresh, no library or homepage generation."""
from refresh_dscr_purchase_methods import refresh
BODY='''<p class="lead">Before buying an STR, price after-hours guest support as an accepted property-level service, not just a monthly subscription. A low messaging fee can leave the buyer responsible for late-night decisions, local attendance and repair bills. Compare the coverage window, incident definition, authority and excluded work on the same candidate address before approving the acquisition budget. The question is not how to become a better host: it is whether the operating arrangement you intend to buy is available, affordable and complete enough for this purchase.</p>
<p><a href="/apply/">Bring the support proposal and complete launch budget to an acquisition discussion</a>. BNB Accelerator assists within its signed acquisition scope; this page does not promise ongoing guest support. It is commercially interested, document-based educational analysis, not firsthand service testing or a provider ranking.</p>
<h2>What to verify before buying: acknowledgment is not resolution</h2>
<p>Keep the original six checks visible: coverage hours, response metrics, escalation tree, property-data access, per-incident charges and refund authority. Ask what each metric measures. Acknowledging a message, contacting the owner, dispatching someone and completing a repair are different events. Obtain the actual proposal's definitions, limitations and handoff, including what happens when the buyer is unavailable. No universal response time or guaranteed repair is supplied here.</p>
<p>For a remote or high-income buyer, paid support can be useful when the contract covers the tasks the buyer cannot reliably perform. It can also be a poor fit if every consequential decision returns to an unavailable owner. Give each candidate service the same property details, intended opening date, time zone, guest-access method and unresolved local arrangements. Do not send seller or guest personal information that is unnecessary for a proposal.</p>
<h2>Original support-quote acceptance worksheet</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Purchase field</th><th>Written evidence to request</th><th>Unresolved decision</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Coverage</td><td>Time zone, daily windows, holidays, language and unavailable periods</td><td>Who handles contacts outside the paid window?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Incident unit</td><td>Definition, included volume, duplicate contacts and overage calculation</td><td>Does one unresolved guest issue generate several billable events?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Authority</td><td>Buyer-approved spending, refund and reservation-change rules</td><td>What requires an owner or other authorized person's decision?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Local response</td><td>Named accepted partner, address coverage, dispatch fee and exclusions</td><td>Who attends when the remote desk cannot resolve the issue?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Access and records</td><td>Permitted platform role, necessary property facts and incident report</td><td>Can the service do the agreed task without passwords or excess access?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Launch acceptance</td><td>Start date, onboarding conditions, invoice example and backup</td><td>Is this address accepted before the buyer relies on coverage?</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>This is an original request list, not a claim that a particular vendor provides every field. Have the actual parties and qualified advisers resolve authority and contract questions. A referral, software login or paid invoice alone does not prove that a local contractor has accepted attendance. Use the <a href="/guides/str-service-responsibility-matrix/">responsibility matrix</a> to keep acquisition coordination separate from continuing operation.</p>
<h2>How to make the decision: test three agreed scenarios</h2>
<p>Before relying on a quote, ask the proposed service to describe three hypothetical paths: a guest cannot use the entry instructions, a reported property issue requires a local assessment, and a guest requests a reservation change or refund outside the service's authority. For each, request the named next person, contact path, approval step, billable events and completion record. Do this as a scheduled tabletop discussion, not a fake guest incident or emergency.</p>
<p>For real emergencies, follow appropriate emergency procedures and contact emergency services where needed; this guide is not a safety protocol. A pre-purchase exercise tests the clarity of a proposed workflow, not future response reliability. If a required local partner has not accepted the address, keep that row unresolved and price a genuinely available alternative rather than declaring the property rental-ready.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.airbnb.com/help/article/1534">Airbnb's co-host permissions guidance</a>, reviewed October 7, 2026, distinguishes platform access from the help separately agreed with a co-host. Calendar-and-messaging access permits guest messaging and calendar viewing, not calendar editing; broader actions require different permissions. Check the current role against the agreed task. This platform description does not establish a provider's service level or property availability. Do not request an owner's password or grant broader access merely to avoid clarifying authority.</p>
<h2>Purchase scenario: compare two invented invoices</h2>
<p><strong>Hypothetical arithmetic only—not vendor prices, market averages, actual incident rates or client results.</strong> Assume proposal A charges $300 monthly plus $40 per incident, and proposal B charges $650 monthly plus $15 per incident. Stipulate for this calculation only that their incident definitions and remote duties are identical, with no included incidents, minimums or taxes. Both exclude local attendance, which costs another invented $180 per dispatch under the same separate accepted arrangement.</p>
<p>At eight incidents and two dispatches, A costs $300 + (8 × $40) + (2 × $180) = $980; B costs $650 + (8 × $15) + (2 × $180) = $1,130. At twenty incidents with the same two dispatches, A costs $1,460 and B costs $1,310. The lower base price wins the first comparison but not the second. At fourteen incidents, the remote subtotals are both $860; adding the same $360 dispatch gives $1,220 each. That crossover is a property of these invented prices, not a prediction of guest demand.</p>
<p>Replace every input with dated proposals and supported assumptions for the address. A higher-priced service may include tasks excluded elsewhere; identical totals do not prove equivalent quality. Count the same dispatch once, and add repair labor or parts separately only where they are distinct obligations. Compare a co-host, manager or buyer-operated alternative on accepted coverage and complete costs through the <a href="/blog/str-property-management-fees/">management-fee worksheet</a>, not on labels.</p>
<h2>Carry the chosen quote into purchase cash</h2>
<p><strong>Separate invented launch budget:</strong> $250,000 is accessible and a complete $234,000 acquisition-and-setup allocation already includes $35,000 retained operating cash but excludes this support arrangement. The initially unallocated margin is $16,000. Add a distinct $900 onboarding payment and earmark three months of A's eight-incident/two-dispatch case, $2,940. Total allocation is $237,840, leaving $12,160 outside the plan. The retained reserve is counted once, not deducted again as an expense.</p>
<p>If one of those three months instead uses the twenty-incident case, replace one $980 month with $1,460: add $480, not another full $1,460. The allocation becomes $238,320 and margin $11,680. These earmarked amounts are not all paid on day one, but their actual due dates matter. They omit repair bills, other changes and receipts; they neither establish adequate reserves nor profit. If coverage costs were already inside the original $234,000, reconcile that component instead of adding them twice. Use the <a href="/tools/first-str-purchase-budget/">purchase cash planner</a> for the full dated case.</p>
<h2>The underwriting mistake to avoid</h2>
<p>Do not buy messaging coverage while assuming physical response, refund authority or full management comes with it. Proceed when the address is accepted, consequential duties and exclusions are understood, professional approvals remain separate and the complete cash case fits. Reprice a viable purchase when verified costs change, or seek valid contract protection while decisive evidence is missing. An unanswered support question does not automatically extend an offer deadline or create a cancellation right.</p>
<p>The <a href="/guides/kleer-circle-year-one-operations/">year-one availability acceptance guide</a> tests whether a particular operating model fits the owner's schedule; this article isolates paid incident pricing and purchase cash. Follow the <a href="/blog/buy-str-high-income-large-tax-bill/">high-income buyer roadmap</a> and <a href="/apply/">discuss the candidate with its actual coverage proposal</a>. Keep guest and seller records in authorized secure channels. Educational only, not legal, tax, lending or personalized investment advice. No response time, safety, continuity, revenue, tax benefit or return is guaranteed.</p>'''
FAQ=[('Does after-hours messaging coverage include local response?','Not automatically. Request the accepted address, local partner, duties, authority, availability and separate charges. A messaging subscription or referral is not evidence that attendance is included.'),('How should an STR buyer compare incident-based support quotes?','Use the same incident definition, period, included duties and dispatch assumptions. Calculate both a supported ordinary case and a labeled stress case, and reconcile exclusions before choosing.'),('Should a support retainer be added to purchase reserves twice?','No. Identify whether it is already allocated, then include distinct onboarding and dated operating commitments once. Retained cash is not a second recurring expense; the complete plan still needs independent review.')]
if __name__=='__main__':
 refresh('after-hours-guest-support-cost','How Should After-Hours Guest Support Be Staffed and Priced | BNB Accelerator','How Should After-Hours Guest Support Be Staffed and Priced?',
 'Before buying an STR, compare after-hours support scope, incident fees and local dispatch. Use a worked quote and launch-cash worksheet.',BODY,FAQ,'2026-09-23','data-after-hours-cost-review-link','Price the <a href="/blog/after-hours-guest-support-cost/">after-hours support quote and launch-cash worksheet</a> before relying on an operating arrangement. Messaging coverage is not accepted local response.')
